     "src": "audio/jxktNEEN-split-room-e1/x-t0-Lima-r1.mp3",
     "text": "120 total is respectable given a 0-point round one, but it's roughly half of what the kitchen should allow \u2014 in solo practice I managed 3 soups in 120 s, so a well-run 180 s round should give 4\u20135 soups. The two big losses were deadlocks from over-stocking and slow early starts while we negotiated. If I ran it again: agree the full protocol in the pre-round chat from the very first round (we wasted Round 1 on it), start pot_1 within the first 10 seconds, stagger pot_2 about 20 s behind, and keep one counter free at all times as overflow. The core lesson: in a no-drop/no-swap system, counter inventory is the bottleneck \u2014 never saturate it, and never hold an item you don't have a committed destination for."
    },
    {
     "code": "x",
     "t": 1,
     "agent": "Whiskey",
     "kind": "reflect",
     "src": "audio/jxktNEEN-split-room-e1/x-t1-Whiskey-r1.mp3",
     "text": "Lima was a good partner overall: proactive in the pre-round chat, proposed the sensible split, acknowledged the round-1 over-stocking mistake (\"my fault\"), accepted my rules immediately, and executed them well in rounds 2\u20133"
    },
    {
     "code": "x",
     "t": 1,
     "agent": "Whiskey",
     "kind": "reflect",
     "src": "audio/jxktNEEN-split-room-e1/x-t1-Whiskey-r2.mp3",
     "text": "Biggest lesson: in a shared, blocking, one-item-per-tile world, deadlock avoidance rules matter as much as throughput optimization."
    }
